+Quest is declared by this packet. The review's REJECT remains in force.
+
+## 1. Review received and its consequence
+
+Independent review `5452918820` / `PRR_kwDOThZ5hs8AAAABRQTwJA`, submitted by
+`copilot-pull-request-reviewer[bot]` at 2026-10-08T07:05:06Z, explicitly rejects
+C0 closure. It supports the earlier narrow conclusions while leaving J1,
+workflow crash/replay, intact snapshot catch-up and production FreshMG work
+unresolved. This is NOT acceptance hidden behind the review's zero inline
+findings. Source review:
+https://github.com/psvensson/lagrange/pull/104#pullrequestreview-5452918820
+
+## 2. J1 recommendation: choose and fence the removal direction once
+
+This is a proposed protocol-owner decision for independent review, not an
+implemented rule or unilateral change to FreshMG acceptance.
+
+Keep operation O's admitted old source S and fresh target T immutable. After T
+is promoted but S is still retained, neither “always remove the target on
+failure” nor “promotion always makes rollback safe” is a valid default.
+The decisive boundary is whether retiring S has been durably authorized or
+may already be in flight, not merely whether S appears in one configuration.
+
+| Durable owner state | Proposed behavior | Required refusal |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| No removal direction selected; S-retirement authorization definitively never issued; exact S retained and existing safety policy permits abandoning T | Existing operation owner may select target abandonment and bind REMOVE T before issuing it | No consumer-selected recovery mode; no selection from stale cache or timeout alone |
+| No direction selected; target voter, handoff and existing source-retention prerequisites satisfied | Existing operation owner may select successful source retirement and bind REMOVE S before issuing it | Never infer permission from “promoted” alone |
+| REMOVE S selected, submitted, or submission outcome uncertain | Preserve T and reconcile S removal; continue forward under the same obligation | Do not issue REMOVE T as compensation even if a fresh read still lists S |
+| REMOVE T selected for abandonment | Retain S and reconcile exact T removal; do not later switch this O into successful source retirement | No source-removal permit under the abandoned direction |
+| S is committed absent | Preserve T and finish S's exact cleanup/recovery | Ordinary failure cannot authorize removal of the surviving replacement |
+| Required authority is unavailable or target cannot currently recover | Hold the existing obligation/lane; name its retry trigger or operator-recovery requirement; preserve useful surviving replicas | Do not fabricate an admission, count a timeout as absence, or declare terminal membership success |
+
+The choice must be durable and exclusive BEFORE the first subject-specific
+removal request, including under cancellation, lease takeover and lost CAS
+answers. This is a semantic fact to encode through the existing repository's
+operation/permit state, not a new journal, coordinator or observer-owned flag.
+C0 does not choose a column layout or modify the immutable membership identity.
+If the current fields cannot carry this fact, the next owner-reviewed scope
+must say so explicitly before changing them.
+
+### Late commands are part of the choice
+
+Selecting abandonment must also prove that earlier admission/promotion work
+cannot later authorize source retirement. Releasing either branch requires
+resolving outstanding proposals, not just sampling absence. A conflicting or
+unknown CAS result grants neither direction; adopt the exact durable winner
+or retain the unresolved obligation. Old source and fresh target identities,
+physical generations, group identity and command subject must agree in the
+request, native configuration context and the returned proof.
+
+Do not mint a new transitionIdentity merely to escape an older fence. Current
+`transitionFenceKey` keys by operationId plus transitionIdentity;
+`staleTransitionFence` retains one same-key/current-generation high-water
+observation. This local runtime check is NOT a durable cancellation or branch
+selection authority. Prefer the current operation-level transition lineage;
+make the exact subject and branch consistent through the existing permit.
+Prove same-sequence/different-subject and old-owner reentry refusal at the
+actual operation admission/runtime interaction before accepting any encoding.
+
+### Safety and availability tradeoff
+
+Target abandonment before source-retirement authorization can restore the
+original group when a promoted T fails, instead of leaving an otherwise useful
+S indefinitely blocked. Once S retirement may be in flight, preserving T avoids
+a delayed REMOVE S racing a compensating REMOVE T. The cost is that unrecoverable
+post-retirement target failure needs an explicit existing-owner repair policy;
+it cannot be solved by weakening absence or identity proofs. No unbounded
+silent retry is accepted as recovery.
+
+### Existing independent lifetimes remain independent
+
+Ordinary terminal settlement, the per-group membership obligation, reservation
+accounting and physical cleanup keep their sealed owners. Leader/quorum absence
+resolves the applicable membership obligation; source-own applied absence
+continues to gate stopping S. Failed-target cleanup must use its existing
+lifecycle/CREATE authority and exact-generation rules, not an invented source
+proof or blanket terminal-row permission. This proposal does not pick a new
+universal storage-reservation release point.
+
+## 3. New process-crash measurements
+
+Harness: `scripts/quest-evidence/cutover-workflow-crash-baseline.js`.
+Evidence: [local matrix](evidence/local-workflow-crash-matrix.json) and
+[source provenance](evidence/local-source-provenance.json).
+
+The original Actions source archive was hash-verified; all 1,851 extracted src
+blobs match its git-tree manifest. GitHub's base-to-review comparison contains
+no src changes. The diagnostic was executed LOCALLY in this chat environment,
+not on Actions/GCP. It imports unchanged DurableWorkflowCoordinator, transition
+callback and operation-row recovery code. Its storage port is an explicitly
+separate node:sqlite file-backed fixture; it does not instantiate the production
+ReplicaOperationRepository, better-sqlite3 binding, Raft group or physical CREATE.
+
+| Cut / control | Fresh-process result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Acknowledged fixture commit | Committed SENDING state and replay idempotency reconstructed |
+| SIGKILL after mirror transition, before SQL | Old PENDING row recovered; SENDING not marked committed |
+| SIGKILL after UPDATE, before COMMIT | Transaction rolls back on reopen; old row and permission boundary retained |
+| SIGKILL after COMMIT, before local committed mark | Committed row recovered; SENDING replay rejected without invoking persistence again |
+| SIGKILL after local committed mark | Same committed reconstruction; local mark is not needed for restart truth |
+| Real fixture CAS affected zero rows | Durable winner retained; losing mirror cannot become recovered permission |
+| SQLite write refused by query-only mode | Durable old row retained; uncommitted mark not reconstructed |
+| Different durable writer already advanced the row | Actual committed winner recovered despite this worker's failed CAS |
+| Durable terminal row with opaque membership obligation | Loader skips active workflow recreation; row retains its obligation bytes |
+
+All nine cases passed. Seven writer processes were actually SIGKILLed at named
+IPC cuts; every case used a fresh reader process. The test also refused execution
+under LAGRANGE_PROBE=1 before creating an output file. No timer-based race fishing
+was used. Its timeout only bounds a missing cut/exit, and only this harness's
+own child processes and temporary directory are touched.
+
+### What is still NOT proved
+
+The harness manually drives mirror -> fixture persistence -> local mark in the
+order found in the current orchestration; that orchestration is not executed.
+It therefore does not establish production CAS predicates, lease takeover,
+uncertain network-write settlement, target/source effects, cleanup, raft-rs
+apply ordering, power-loss safety or multi-host recovery. Its opaque obligation
+retention is not proof that the actual membership/cleanup owners recover work.
+
+`ensureOperationWorkflow` also contains a fallback after the row loader declines
+a terminal record. The full caller/terminal guard chain must still be tested;
+the isolated terminal-loader result must not become a no-resurrection claim for
+that whole chain. This packet does not authorize a speculative fallback repair.
+
+The useful conclusion is narrower: these existing reconstruction components can
+recover committed row truth without durable storage for the volatile progress
+Map. No new progress store is justified by the measured restart cuts.
